For documentation and instructions for building this software,
see <http://www.live555.com/liveMedia/>

NOTE: You should download the "LIVE555 Streaming Media" software only from the official site:
    http://www.live555.com/liveMedia/

Old versions of the code, and versions of the code from other sites
(including 'GitHub' repositories) are NOT SUPPORTED, and should not be used.
For more information, see:
    http://live555.com/liveMedia/faq.html#latest-version


---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Optional deadline-aware queued RTP-over-TCP send mode (RTPInterface)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

What it is
~~~~~~~~~~
By default an RTPInterface sends each RTP/RTCP packet inline. For a low-latency
player that multiplexes several audio/video channels over one plaintext TCP
connection, back pressure (a slow reader) can make that a problem. An explicit
opt-in "queued send mode" replaces the inline send with a per-connection FIFO
that is SHARED by every RTPInterface interleaving onto the same socket.

In queued mode sendPacket() copies the complete RFC 2326 section 10.12 frame
  '$' + channel + uint16 length + payload
into the queue and returns success meaning "copied and taken over"; the caller
may immediately overwrite its own buffer. Frames are emitted in submission
order through a non-blocking writer. A frame whose play deadline has passed
before its FIRST byte is sent is dropped. Once even one byte (header or body)
of a frame has been written, that frame is always completed: a later frame is
never concatenated onto a truncated body, regardless of expiry, channel
removal, or newer packets.

Connection applicability (the application owns this contract)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Use queued mode ONLY for an application-managed, plaintext TCP connection that
carries RTP/RTCP only. It is deliberately NOT extended to:
  * TLS connections (enableQueuedSendMode() refuses a TLSState-marked socket),
  * SRTP (the queue sees already-encrypted packets; do not enable it there),
  * RTSP control responses / any connection using a server-request
    alternative-byte handler. Enabling queued mode on such a socket, or
    registering an alternative-byte handler after queued mode is enabled, is
    refused (and logged) so the two traffic kinds are never silently mixed.
UDP and sockets for which the mode was not enabled keep the existing behaviour.

How to enable
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
1. Attach the channel(s) to the socket first:
       rtpInterface.addStreamSocket(sockNum, rtpChannel, NULL /*tlsState*/);
       rtcpInterface.addStreamSocket(sockNum, rtcpChannel, NULL);
2. Enable the shared queue with a NON-ZERO byte budget (bytes not yet handed
   to the underlying writer):
       RTPInterface::enableQueuedSendMode(env, sockNum, byteLimit);
   It fails (state unchanged) when the socket is unknown, already queued, has
   a zero budget, has an alternative-byte handler, or is TLS. Over-budget
   packets are rejected (sendPacket() returns False); earlier fresh packets are
   never evicted to make room. A payload larger than 65535 bytes is rejected so
   no truncated 16-bit length header is ever emitted.
3. Optionally give each MEDIA interface a media clock before its first packet:
       rtpInterface.setPlaybackTiming(clockRate, baseRtpTimestamp,
                                      localBaseUs, playBufferUs);
   clockRate must be positive. An interface left unconfigured (e.g. RTCP) has
   no play deadline but still shares the same FIFO.

Clock units and the deadline formula
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
All local time values are MONOTONIC MICROSECONDS (the default clock reads
CLOCK_MONOTONIC). The media clockRate is in Hz. A frame's deadline is
       localBaseUs + playBufferUs
         + floor( int32_t(rtpTimestamp - baseRtpTimestamp) * 1000000 / clockRate )
The timestamp difference is reinterpreted as a SIGNED 32-bit value, so a single
continuous segment less than half a cycle apart is handled correctly across the
32-bit wrap, including negative deltas (floor, not truncation). Fragments with
the same RTP timestamp get the same deadline. A frame already at or past its
deadline when submitted is rejected and never takes queue capacity.

Scheduling and the replaceable test hooks
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
The queue plugs into the existing TaskScheduler, not a polling sleep: the
socket's background handler gains SOCKET_WRITABLE only while a frame is queued,
and a one-shot delayed task is armed for the nearest unstarted frame's deadline.
There is no blocking send on this path; short writes continue at the offset,
EINTR consumes nothing and is retried, EAGAIN parks until writable, and any
other error terminates only this connection's queue and removes its callbacks
(other sockets are unaffected). Removing a channel withdraws only its frames
that have not started; in-flight frames are completed. When the last interface
leaves the socket, all tasks and state are released so a reused fd cannot be
reached by stale callbacks.

For deterministic memory replay, three entry points are replaceable:
  * setQueuedSendHooks(): a clock function (monotonic microseconds) and an
    underlying non-blocking write function, both with client data;
  * pumpQueuedSend(): the single-step pump (expire due frames, then write);
  * the default implementations use the real monotonic clock and a
    non-blocking send().
See task_tests/queued_send_example.cpp for a compilable end-to-end example and
task_tests/deadline_tcp.cpp for the scripted short-write/EAGAIN/EINTR/hard-error
replay tests (framing, deadlines and lifecycle groups).
